gmcquary@Ingres.COM (George F. McQuary) writes:

>You see a major spoiler ahead.


[Hell is boring, so just wish for the Amulet on the castle level and 
escape.]

You don't think the high priest of Moloch is going to set up shop with
the most powerful artifact in the universe on level one, do you?  Hell
is designed to keep people out.  In fact, it asks you if you really
want to enter.  And after all this time, the priest has enchanted the
Amulet with a spell of return to make it drag its possessor back to him.

Logical framework aside, it would be too trivial to win the game by
wishing for it and ascending.  Or impossible, depending on how you
look at it.  If you don't get the Bell of Opening, you can never gain
the levels needed to deal with the Elemental Planes.  That means you
have to descend deep into Gehennom anyway.  Might as well fetch the 
Amulet while you're there.  And there IS a way to prevent it from
teleporting you back levels, at least, less often than it does normally.

Later on, you suggested having various quests and giving the player the
option of deciding which one to go on.  That's okay, but I prefer
having one ueber-quest, an overriding goal to achieve.  In Omega, there
are various tasks to do, but the final goal is not all-important--
you can twaddle around the countryside forever if you feel like it.
Omega is therefore more boring due in part to this fact.  

By the way, you can get the real thing in debug mode because it's debug
mode--in it, you should be able to recreate any situation someone 
describes to you when the game crashed.  So, if carrying around the
Amulet causes the game to seg fault, the programmers had better be
able to figure out why without having to wander down to Hell to fetch
it.  :)
